<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="refrigerator-repair/lg-ice-maker-not-working"
  eyebrow="LG Refrigerator Ice Maker · Diagnostic Guide · Same Day Southern California"
  title="LG Refrigerator Not Making Ice? Check the Temp, Then Reset"
  subtitle="Most LG no-ice calls come down to the filter, supply, or a freezer that's not cold enough. Here's LG's own Test-button and reset steps, Craft Ice notes, and when it's a tech job."
  heroImageTitle="LG Ice Maker Not Working Repair"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Water path, temperature, then LG's Test button.</h2>

    <p><strong>When an LG refrigerator stops making ice, check the water path first: the filter (replace every six months — a clogged one starves the maker), the supply valve and line, a frozen fill tube, and whether the ice maker is on. LG is specific on temperature — the freezer needs to be below about 19°F (with -4°F recommended) to run the maker, so a warm freezer is a common cause. Then use LG's own steps: hold Ice On/Off 3 to 5 seconds to confirm it's on, and press the Test button (a visible button or a small pinhole you reach with a paperclip) to force a harvest. If the water path and temperature are fine and a reset doesn't help, the inlet valve or ice maker assembly is the fault — a tech job. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p>This page covers the ice maker built into your LG refrigerator. For a standalone or undercounter machine, use the <a href="/services/ice-maker-repair/">ice maker repair</a> hub; for fridge ice makers of any brand, the <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/ice-maker-issues/">refrigerator ice maker issues</a> guide is the umbrella.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 RESET (differentiator) -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · LG's own reset steps</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Test button and reset, LG's way.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Confirm it's on</h3>
      <p>Hold the <strong>Ice On/Off</strong> button for 3 to 5 seconds until "Ice On" shows, or check the physical switch on the left side of the ice maker. An accidentally-off maker is a common no-ice cause.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Force a harvest with the Test button</h3>
      <p>Press the <strong>Test button</strong> and hold about 3 seconds until the tray or arms turn. Some LG models have a visible button; others use a small pinhole you reach with a straightened paperclip. If it cycles but no water arrives, the valve is the issue; if nothing moves, it's the ice maker.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Full reset</h3>
      <p>Remove the ice bin, gently shake the ice maker up and down, replace the bin, then turn the refrigerator off for 30 seconds and back on. Allow <strong>4 to 6 hours</strong> for the first ice. On <strong>Craft Ice</strong> models (round, slow-melt ice), unplug for about 10 minutes and then allow up to 24 hours.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why an LG fridge ice maker stops</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, water-first.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Clogged water filter (most common)</h3>
      <p>Good for about six months; past that, flow drops and ice tapers off then stops. Replacing an overdue filter is the single most common fix.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Freezer not cold enough</h3>
      <p>LG wants the freezer below 19°F (ideally -4°F) to cycle the maker. A warm freezer — from a door left ajar, a dirty condenser, or a cooling fault — stops ice production and is worth ruling out early.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Closed supply, kinked line, or frozen fill tube</h3>
      <p>A partly-closed shutoff, a kinked line, or a fill tube frozen into a plug all cut off water to the mold. Confirm the supply and thaw the fill tube gently.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Water inlet valve or ice maker assembly</h3>
      <p>When water is available but the mold never fills, the inlet valve has failed; when the mechanism won't cycle on a Test-button press, the ice maker assembly is the fault. Both are tech diagnoses and replacements.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Run these before calling anyone.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Change the water filter</strong> if it's older than six months.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the freezer temperature</strong> — it should be below 19°F, ideally -4°F. Fix a warm freezer first.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm the supply</strong> — shutoff valve open, line not kinked — and thaw a frozen fill tube gently.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm the ice maker is on</strong> (Ice On/Off held 3–5 seconds) and <strong>run the Test button</strong> to force a harvest.</li>
      <li><strong>Do the full reset</strong> if needed (shake, power off 30 seconds, wait 4–6 hours; Craft Ice: unplug 10 minutes, wait up to 24 hours).</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> filter, temperature, supply, fill-tube thaw, Test button, and reset are safe homeowner work and fix most no-ice calls. Do not replace the water inlet valve or open the ice maker assembly — those are electrically live, plumbed-in tech jobs.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · When it's a tech job</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Reset and water checks done, still no ice? Now it's a diagnostic.</h2>

    <p>When the filter is fresh, the freezer is cold enough, the supply is open, and LG's reset doesn't restore ice, the fault is the water inlet valve or the ice maker assembly. Our techs run the Test button to isolate which, verify the water path, and replace only the failed part. In LA's hard water we also clear mineral scale off the inlet-valve screen and fill tube, a common no-cost flow fix. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>

    <p>More help: the <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/ice-maker-issues/">refrigerator ice maker issues</a> umbrella (all brands), the full <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/">refrigerator repair</a> hub, and for a standalone unit, <a href="/services/ice-maker-repair/not-making-ice/">ice maker not making ice</a>. Brand coverage: <a href="/brands/lg-refrigerator-repair/">LG refrigerator repair</a> and <a href="/brands/lg-ice-maker-repair/">LG ice maker repair</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/refrigerator-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/refrigerator-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/irvine/refrigerator-repair/">Irvine</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 FAQ -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">LG ice maker not working: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 BOTTOM CTA -->
<section class="bottom-cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>LG still not making ice after a reset? Call today.</h2>
    <p>$89 diagnostic waived with repair. Same-day across LA, OC, Ventura. Phones answered 24/7.</p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href={phoneHref} class="btn-primary">☎ Call {phone}</a>
      <a href="/book/" class="btn-secondary">Book Online</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

